{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{2{x}^{3/2}}{3}+C) & = \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{2{x}^{3/2}}{3})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) & = \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3/2})+0 & = \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}{x}^{3/2-1} & = \\\\ {x}^{1/2}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503893 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "85af4ca3-5e66-522a-9e5a-6efb1c7114ba" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Antiderivative of a Radical Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "63820451-3b6f-5309-9075-931645a73f51" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2{x}^{11/2}}{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503888}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "63820451-3b6f-5309-9075-931645a73f51" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2{x}^{11/2}}{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503888} :answer-field/id #uuid "2abeee87-15e4-50b2-9c14-428aff34385c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503884}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8c4e1271-e72c-5666-b995-18dcd8b13de0" :question/problem "Find the antiderivative of $\\sqrt[2]{{x}^{9}}.$\n\nWork on $x>0$.\n\nThe antiderivative is {{field-1}} $+C$. Enter only the term before $+C$, with no additive constant." :question/worked-solution "We start by writing the root as a rational power, $\\sqrt[2]{{x}^{9}}={x}^{9/2}.$ So, we can apply the power rule with $n=\\frac{9}{2}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\sqrt[2]{{x}^{9}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {x}^{9/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{9/2+1}}{(\\frac{9}{2}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{11/2}}{(\\frac{11}{2})}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{2{x}^{11/2}}{11}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, let's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{2{x}^{11/2}}{11}+C) & = \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{2{x}^{11/2}}{11})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) & = \\\\ \\frac{2}{11}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{11/2})+0 & = \\\\ \\frac{2}{11}\\cdot \\frac{11}{2}{x}^{11/2-1} & = \\\\ {x}^{9/2}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503894 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "85af4ca3-5e66-522a-9e5a-6efb1c7114ba" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Antiderivative of a Radical Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b6ceeab1-a236-5548-8ed0-24d60c041807" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5{x}^{12/5}}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503908}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b6ceeab1-a236-5548-8ed0-24d60c041807" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5{x}^{12/5}}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503908} :answer-field/id #uuid "29a5b88e-4517-5fae-a7e6-afb8d22124ab" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503883}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8c582689-ffe0-5d18-bc8e-7ca7d9abd1e2" :question/problem "Find the antiderivative of $\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{7}}.$\n\nWork on $x>0$.\n\nThe antiderivative is {{field-1}} $+C$. Enter only the term before $+C$, with no additive constant." :question/worked-solution "We start by writing the root as a rational power, $\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{7}}={x}^{7/5}.$ So, we can apply the power rule with $n=\\frac{7}{5}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\sqrt[5]{{x}^{7}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {x}^{7/5}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{7/5+1}}{(\\frac{7}{5}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{12/5}}{(\\frac{12}{5})}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{5{x}^{12/5}}{12}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, let's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{5{x}^{12/5}}{12}+C) & = \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{5{x}^{12/5}}{12})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) & = \\\\ \\frac{5}{12}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{12/5})+0 & = \\\\ \\frac{5}{12}\\cdot \\frac{12}{5}{x}^{12/5-1} & = \\\\ {x}^{7/5}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503896 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "85af4ca3-5e66-522a-9e5a-6efb1c7114ba" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Antiderivative of a Radical Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a59f9be0-20c3-5247-aa01-a44a64642023" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3{x}^{5/3}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503905}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a59f9be0-20c3-5247-aa01-a44a64642023" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3{x}^{5/3}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503905} :answer-field/id #uuid "ba3c18ab-e570-5bd7-ba7b-e0fe86f89702" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503909}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9872673e-ba0e-5ee1-acfb-78f028930b51" :question/problem "Find the antiderivative of ${x}^{2/3}.$\n\nWork on $x>0$.\n\nThe antiderivative is {{field-1}} $+C$. Enter only the term before $+C$, with no additive constant." :question/worked-solution "We can apply the power rule with $n=\\frac{2}{3}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{2/3}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{{x}^{2/3+1}}{(\\frac{2}{3}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{5/3}}{(\\frac{5}{3})}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{3{x}^{5/3}}{5}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, let's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{3{x}^{5/3}}{5}+C) & = \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{3{x}^{5/3}}{5})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) & = \\\\ \\frac{3}{5}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{5/3})+0 & = \\\\ \\frac{3}{5}\\cdot \\frac{5}{3}{x}^{5/3-1} & = \\\\ {x}^{2/3}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503897 :knowl